-- Q13821: Government Regulation of Business [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Restatement (Third) of Agency Section 7.07, respondeat superior holds an employer vicariously liable for torts committed within the scope of employment. Courts distinguish between a minor detour, where the employer remains liable, and a complete departure or frolic, where liability shifts to the employee alone.
Wrong (A): Express permission is relevant but not dispositive; an unauthorized act may still fall within the scope of employment if it constitutes only a minor deviation.
Wrong (C): Insurance coverage is irrelevant to the legal determination of vicarious liability under respondeat superior.
Wrong (D): The third party''s knowledge of the employment relationship does not affect the respondeat superior analysis, which focuses on the agent''s scope of duties.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 13821;

-- Q13822: Government Regulation of Business [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under UCC Section 9-310(a), filing a financing statement perfects a security interest and establishes the creditor''s priority over most subsequent claimants. It provides constructive notice and determines the order of claims under UCC Section 9-322.
Wrong (A): The security agreement, not the financing statement, creates the security interest between the parties under UCC Section 9-203.
Wrong (B): The financing statement is a public notice document for perfection purposes and does not satisfy the statute of frauds for the underlying loan agreement.
Wrong (D): Filing does not guarantee full recovery; it establishes priority position, but recovery depends on the value of the collateral at the time of default.$EXPL$
