-- Q13825: Government Regulation of Business [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Restatement (Third) of Agency Section 2.06, when an undisclosed principal authorizes an agent to enter contracts, both the agent and the principal are liable to third parties. Upon discovering the principal, the third party may elect to hold either party responsible for performance.
Wrong (A): The agent is not solely liable; once the undisclosed principal is discovered, both are bound and the third party may choose whom to pursue.
Wrong (C): The principal is not always solely liable; in undisclosed principal situations, the agent who contracted in their own name also remains personally liable.
Wrong (D): Concealment of the agency does not void the contract; the undisclosed principal doctrine specifically addresses this situation and preserves enforceability.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 13825;

-- Q13826: Government Regulation of Business [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under Restatement (Third) of Agency Section 3.07, the death of the principal terminates the agent''s authority by operation of law, regardless of whether the agent or third parties have notice. Contracts entered after the principal''s death do not bind the estate.
Wrong (A): Good faith and lack of knowledge do not preserve authority that has been extinguished by operation of law upon the principal''s death.
Wrong (B): The contract is not merely voidable; the agent lacked authority entirely, so the contract cannot bind the deceased principal''s estate.
Wrong (D): The supplier''s lack of notice does not create enforceable rights against the estate; death terminates authority automatically regardless of third-party knowledge.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 13826;

